**Definition 13.3 (unchanged source service and normalized return).** In
the new dispatcher, a source frame is first actually acquired by Definition
13.2. Clone the entire original per-frame fragment from its reserve row
at old Ready through its final acknowledged return, with fresh finite PC
names. Replace each original public frame IN row by RD(38,that same input
latch); MR(38), on the same raw frame bits at their actual start. Give
that RD equal successors, preserving the original IN's lack of a test.
Redirect the original fragment's final return edge to the new common
return; do not enter another old-frame reserve. This replacement is a
finite two-row expansion; no source-dependent reply is submitted by it.
The old ingress retention, parsing and context conversion really execute
again on their original ports. Their cells and receipts are additional
actual storage/work, distinct from the new port-38 retention. It is not
the row interpreter Step. All producer and private-source rows execute
on the actual original source ports.

Before that path, position the actual frame reader at its start using
LastBank(38). After it, finish all original mandatory source commitment,
counter/row construction, copy and acknowledgement. Original native
control is cloned at finite renamed states. At each source row writer,
replace only its old fixed version-field constructor with the following
fixed finite word: the original $v_0$ tag, the literal bit tag 10100101, and
$U(37)U(0)$ naming the code-capture field's actual port and first descriptor.
The capture is the first block descriptor on the formerly unused 36;
its creation scan actually constructs ordinal zero. The fixed pointer is
therefore proved to name that captured installed-description block, not
an arbitrary asserted transcript. Its descriptor contains the acquired
actual base/length and authentic creation chain. The version word itself
is emitted by literal Append rows; its finite framed length is emitted
by the same fixed finite construction. This embeds neither $D_1$ nor
its bit length in $D_1$. The installation predecessor is the version's
manifest binding; there is only one activation in this version.

Keep the original row prefix and its old version fields immutable. New
rows' fences, descriptors and source-counter fields are their actual
new-run ones. The original native row-length arithmetic consumes this
new field's actual framed length in place of the old field's length;
all other fields and that arithmetic's loops are unchanged. When a total
row length is constructed, use those actual lengths before allocation.
No old fixed version-width bound is reused. This adapter uses literal
appends and the existing native row writer, not a word-program call
inside an original fragment. It has no bank or service-scratch effect,
no import of partly built private rows, and no new return-address
convention. Public frame material supplied
to it is bit for bit its original canonical frame; TagRead is not replaced
by a semantic answer and reject is not bypassed. All original auxiliary
heads on ports 0–31 are as at their previous source-ready return; the word
compiler touches only 32–39; service receiver 40 is separate from those
source-frame arenas. Mailbox setup always starts with its actual Base and
